WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.080 --> 00:00:04.270
Film Courage: Let’s talk about the hypothetical
spec script, let’s suppose I want to get

00:00:04.270 --> 00:00:07.270
on a show, what would be my method for doing
this?

00:00:07.270 --> 00:00:09.800
Am I writing an episode that already exists?

00:00:09.800 --> 00:00:11.960
A continuation of one?

00:00:11.960 --> 00:00:17.369
Erik Bork, Author/Screenwriter: In television
these days to be in consideration for getting

00:00:17.369 --> 00:00:22.240
your first writing job on staff of a show
which means you didn’t sell your own original

00:00:22.240 --> 00:00:26.400
show, you got hired to write for someone else’s
show which is still the much more likely scenario

00:00:26.400 --> 00:00:28.400
for a new writer, right?

00:00:28.400 --> 00:00:32.422
People are writing original pilots and hoping
to sell them and get them on in front of an

00:00:32.422 --> 00:00:38.180
audience of course there’s always a chance
that that could happen but it’s very rare.

00:00:38.180 --> 00:00:43.710
Today people writing original pilots within
the industry, people who have managers and

00:00:43.710 --> 00:00:47.780
agents writing original pilots, if they don’t
already have a big tract record writing for

00:00:47.780 --> 00:00:52.800
television or film and a lot of success, they
are probably writing that as a writing sample

00:00:52.800 --> 00:00:58.260
and trying to get a job on staff of a series,
what’s changed in the 20 years since I was

00:00:58.260 --> 00:01:04.280
doing that is that back in the 90’s and
before, the traditional way to get hired on

00:01:04.280 --> 00:01:09.490
staff of a series was to write what they would
call a spec episode of an existing show.

00:01:09.490 --> 00:01:15.200
Just some successful relatively new show what
the industry liked and respected like SEINFELD

00:01:15.200 --> 00:01:19.490
was the big show to do during its second season
is about the right time.

00:01:19.490 --> 00:01:25.790
Where people are writing their own ideas for
an original SEINFELD that hopefully reads

00:01:25.790 --> 00:01:27.860
like a really good episode of SEINFELD.

00:01:27.860 --> 00:01:30.551
Like you read the script and you’re like
this could be on that show, it could be a

00:01:30.551 --> 00:01:34.810
really good episode of that show, they capture
the voices, it’s very funny, etcetera, etcetera.

00:01:34.810 --> 00:01:36.560
Not easy to do really well.

00:01:36.560 --> 00:01:41.380
Easy to do mediocrely, not easy to do well
enough where people go “Wow!”

00:01:41.380 --> 00:01:45.020
Which of course that’s what you want when
you’re a screenwriter is to get that wow

00:01:45.020 --> 00:01:47.320
is rare and sight after, right?

00:01:47.320 --> 00:01:50.940
So back then very few people wrote original
pilots if they weren’t already established

00:01:50.940 --> 00:01:52.550
because there was nothing to do with them.

00:01:52.550 --> 00:01:55.430
They weren’t looking for original pilots
as writing samples as much.

00:01:55.430 --> 00:01:59.870
They want to know that you can write in another
writer’s voice an episode of show that already

00:01:59.870 --> 00:02:03.950
existed which of course is what you would
be doing if you got hired on staff.

00:02:03.950 --> 00:02:08.479
For some reason in the last 20 years that
has shifted a lot.

00:02:08.479 --> 00:02:12.560
What I’ve heard anecdotally is that where
in those days 80 percent of it was specs of

00:02:12.560 --> 00:02:17.830
existing shows and nowadays it’s like 80
percent of it is original pilots that people

00:02:17.830 --> 00:02:21.700
are writing and that is what is getting the
managers and agents and getting their first

00:02:21.700 --> 00:02:24.530
job on a series (on a writing staff).

00:02:24.530 --> 00:02:26.950
People still do write specs of existing shows.

00:02:26.950 --> 00:02:30.090
I think you definitely need to write them
to get into to some of the big fellowships

00:02:30.090 --> 00:02:33.950
that some of the big studios and networks
offer which are great ways to try and break

00:02:33.950 --> 00:02:35.830
into TV writing.

00:02:35.830 --> 00:02:40.480
But what is much more in vogue is writers
writing original pilots for series that they

00:02:40.480 --> 00:02:46.130
come up with and those pilots become their
main writing sample or samples that they use

00:02:46.130 --> 00:02:51.860
to try to first get a manager and then get
an agent (they kind of have to go step-by-step)

00:02:51.860 --> 00:02:57.480
and then having their managers sending them
to production companies, showrunners, executives

00:02:57.480 --> 00:03:03.680
and the studios and networks, streaming services
to try to get you noticed as a potential TV

00:03:03.680 --> 00:03:04.680
staff writer.

00:03:04.680 --> 00:03:07.910
Film Courage: And so how long would these
spec scripts be?

00:03:07.910 --> 00:03:09.900
So it’s going to be half-hour content?

00:03:09.900 --> 00:03:15.170
Erik: Yes, it’s going to be the same typical
length for the script you’re writing for.

00:03:15.170 --> 00:03:17.920
Well…if it’s original, a show of the ilk
of what you’re writing.

00:03:17.920 --> 00:03:22.180
In other words, if you’re writing a one-hour
(clearly) a network style drama, it’s going

00:03:22.180 --> 00:03:28.190
to be about 45-minutes because there are going
to be commercials and then how many pages

00:03:28.190 --> 00:03:29.460
that translates to.

00:03:29.460 --> 00:03:32.020
They say a minute a page, you can go a little
long.

00:03:32.020 --> 00:03:39.239
In a spec I would say a 1-hour pilot, 60 pages
would probably be the max you’d want to

00:03:39.239 --> 00:03:40.239
go for.

00:03:40.239 --> 00:03:47.200
Whereas a pilot you’re really trying to
copy successful shows that are similar to

00:03:47.200 --> 00:03:49.500
yours would do in terms of length.

00:03:49.500 --> 00:03:55.370
So it’s really helpful to read scripts,
not just read episodes that inspire you to

00:03:55.370 --> 00:04:00.800
want to write something in that genre whether
it’s an HBO-type show or a Netflix-type

00:04:00.800 --> 00:04:01.900
show.

00:04:01.900 --> 00:04:08.330
Nowadays those shows have services that can
have different length shows so that they’re

00:04:08.330 --> 00:04:13.769
not a standard 20-minute half-hour comedy
on ABC there’s a little more flexibility.

00:04:13.769 --> 00:04:19.849
So you don’t have to be too nervous about
the exact right number of pages but generally

00:04:19.849 --> 00:04:23.430
you are emulating what is standard for the
kind of script that you’re writing.

00:04:23.430 --> 00:04:28.419
Film Courage: Why do you say it’s so hard
to make a great spec script, it’s easier

00:04:28.419 --> 00:04:30.110
to make a mediocre one.

00:04:30.110 --> 00:04:31.110
What’s the cause?

00:04:31.110 --> 00:04:36.809
Erik: Well, when you write an existing spec
script of a show, I think it’s easy-ish

00:04:36.809 --> 00:04:41.729
to write something where the characters talk
the way the talk on the show and you have

00:04:41.729 --> 00:04:46.499
the kind of jokes you can imagine they would
have but maybe they are not great jokes but

00:04:46.499 --> 00:04:49.050
kind of okay jokes.

00:04:49.050 --> 00:04:53.770
And the kind of story and behavior that is
their typical behavior.

00:04:53.770 --> 00:04:56.999
It’s not so hard to mimic that.

00:04:56.999 --> 00:05:02.199
It’s hard to mimic in a way that is as good
or better then an actual produced show is.

00:05:02.199 --> 00:05:06.300
Because really for a way for one of those
script to stand out it actually has to be

00:05:06.300 --> 00:05:08.069
better than the average episode.

00:05:08.069 --> 00:05:10.539
It has to feel like “Why haven’t they
done this episode?

00:05:10.539 --> 00:05:14.249
It’s like a great idea, it’s perfect for
the show, it’s really funny, it’s really

00:05:14.249 --> 00:05:15.249
memorable.”

00:05:15.249 --> 00:05:19.509
(If it’s a comedy obviously you are going
for the funny) and that’s just hard to do

00:05:19.509 --> 00:05:20.509
really well.

00:05:20.509 --> 00:05:24.860
It looks easy (which is why I tried to do
it), it looks easy to write a show that you

00:05:24.860 --> 00:05:30.839
know is a good show to come up with your own
ideas for stories for an episode but to do

00:05:30.839 --> 00:05:37.259
it on a level that someone would hire you
based on it is not easy and normally the show

00:05:37.259 --> 00:05:39.360
you wrote it for is not going to hire you.

00:05:39.360 --> 00:05:41.389
They’re probably not even going to want
to read it.

00:05:41.389 --> 00:05:44.759
They probably will be tougher on it than any
other show will be because they know their

00:05:44.759 --> 00:05:46.319
show so well.

00:05:46.319 --> 00:05:49.740
And they probably aren’t looking for writers
because they are already on the air with a

00:05:49.740 --> 00:05:50.740
hit.

00:05:50.740 --> 00:05:53.919
So typically when you’re breaking into television
you’re breaking in with a staff writing

00:05:53.919 --> 00:05:57.659
job on a brand new show that no one has ever
seen before.

00:05:57.659 --> 00:06:01.509
You may not even love the show but it’s
where they offered you the job based on reading

00:06:01.509 --> 00:06:06.499
your stuff and it may be a job that gets canceled
in its first season because most of them do

00:06:06.499 --> 00:06:10.759
but that’s kind of the life of what they
call a baby writer (a writer breaking into

00:06:10.759 --> 00:06:13.059
television working on staff).

00:06:13.059 --> 00:06:17.180
But most of that doesn’t apply as much today
because most people are writing original pilots

00:06:17.180 --> 00:06:18.529
instead of specs.

00:06:18.529 --> 00:06:21.900
But obviously it’s hard to write an original
pilot that impresses people too.

00:06:21.900 --> 00:06:25.860
Perhaps harder because now you’re coming
up with characters and a whole situation that

00:06:25.860 --> 00:06:31.259
you know nothing about and you have to make
them care about these characters be entertained

00:06:31.259 --> 00:06:35.599
and be intrigued and all that kind of stuff
and all of the problems of the show have to

00:06:35.599 --> 00:06:37.119
be really compelling.

00:06:37.119 --> 00:06:40.979
Whereas if you’re writing a spec episode
of an existing show, a lot of that has already

00:06:40.979 --> 00:06:42.410
been done for you.

00:06:42.410 --> 00:06:47.339
You just have to write a variation on something
that someone has already established.

00:06:47.339 --> 00:06:51.149
Maybe that’s why nowadays those aren’t
valued as much because people can say “You

00:06:51.149 --> 00:06:54.249
didn’t have to do as much to do that.”

00:06:54.249 --> 00:06:58.839
On the other hand when you write on staff
of a show a big part of your job is mimicking

00:06:58.839 --> 00:07:00.589
the voice of the showrunner.

00:07:00.589 --> 00:07:04.589
Being able to execute the show in a way that
they would have liked, that they would have

00:07:04.589 --> 00:07:08.550
done it, which is not a skill that every writer
has.

00:07:08.550 --> 00:07:14.559
So to show you can write somebody else’s
show and their voice really I think is a valuable

00:07:14.559 --> 00:07:15.559
thing.

00:07:15.559 --> 00:07:20.889
But the industry is more interested in original
pilots for writing samples despite that I

00:07:20.889 --> 00:07:21.889
think these days.

00:07:21.889 --> 00:07:25.759
Film Courage: But you say it’s rare that…let’s
suppose for SEINFELD, I’m writing to get

00:07:25.759 --> 00:07:29.939
on it and it’s still in existence, that
I’m writing this [for the show] and thinking

00:07:29.939 --> 00:07:32.419
that I’m going to be in the writer’s room
for SEINFELD.

00:07:32.419 --> 00:07:34.220
No…I’m submitting it for FRIENDS?

00:07:34.220 --> 00:07:37.430
Erik: Well, you’re not submitting it for
FRIENDS, you’re submitting it for some show

00:07:37.430 --> 00:07:38.819
that hasn’t even even on the air yet.

00:07:38.819 --> 00:07:43.370
And that’s the reality because your agent
would be the one doing all the submitting,

00:07:43.370 --> 00:07:44.370
right?

00:07:44.370 --> 00:07:46.179
So first you have to impress an agent enough
to get an agent.

00:07:46.179 --> 00:07:51.569
But once you have an agent, they are going
to be submitting these new potential writers,

00:07:51.569 --> 00:07:55.399
all those brand new shows that just found
out they got a series order and now they have

00:07:55.399 --> 00:07:56.509
to staff up.

00:07:56.509 --> 00:07:59.879
The shows have are already on the air, they
already have a staff.

00:07:59.879 --> 00:08:04.060
They may have one person leave and they fill
once vacancy but basically they are already

00:08:04.060 --> 00:08:05.060
there.

00:08:05.060 --> 00:08:10.069
So you’re almost never going to get on a
successful existing hit show that you love

00:08:10.069 --> 00:08:11.779
or that you would have written a spec of.

00:08:11.779 --> 00:08:15.569
You’re going to use that spec to get on
some brand new unknown show that you may or

00:08:15.569 --> 00:08:21.179
may not love or may or may not be right for
but they thought you were right for based

00:08:21.179 --> 00:08:22.439
on your script so there you go.

